%I A399763 #17 Sep 22 2026 17:51:24
%S A399763 1,2,4,8,24,128,648,1351,1278,136,16,48,1926,654,3004,1321,3053,679,
%T A399763 1957,112,282,622,4995,3130,1523,11727,1437,2201,8672,1566,1393,13355,
%U A399763 69506,26318,166630,165036,81004,11951,93498,8980,3521,342996,11610,9677,31296
%N A399763 a(n) = index of first appearance of a building of height n in the city-building sequence A399633.
%C A399763 It is not known if every number is the height of some building. 56 is the smallest unseen height in the first 2.3 billion terms of A399633, thus this sequence is known through a(55).
%C A399763 It has now been proven that every number appears as the height of a building. See the proof by _Rémy Sigrist_ in the comments of A399633. The proof can easily be extended to show that every number appears infinitely many times. - _Eric Fox_, Sep 22 2026
%H A399763 Eric Fox, <a href="/A399763/b399763.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..55</a>
%F A399763 a(n) = min{h >= 1: A399633(h) = n}.
%e A399763 a(8) = 1278 because 1278 is the earliest index n such that A399633(n) = 8.
%Y A399763 Cf. A399633 (left inverse), A397831.
%K A399763 nonn,new
